What is a StatefulSet?

A StatefulSet in Kubernetes is a workload API object used to manage stateful applications.

Unlike Deployments (stateless), StatefulSets maintain:

Stable network identity

Persistent storage

Ordered deployment & scaling


Used when each pod needs a unique identity and data persistence.


Key Features

1. Stable Pod Identity

Pods get fixed names:

myapp-0
myapp-1
myapp-2

These names do not change even after restart.


Stable Network Identity

Each pod gets a DNS hostname:

myapp-0.myservice.namespace.svc.cluster.local

Requires a Headless Service (clusterIP: None)


Persistent Storage

Each pod gets its own Persistent Volume (PV) via PVC.
Storage is not deleted when pod restarts.


4. Ordered Deployment & Scaling

Pods are created in sequence:

myapp-0 → myapp-1 → myapp-2


Deletion happens in reverse order.


5. Ordered Rolling Updates

Updates happen one pod at a time (safe for databases).



StatefulSet vs Deployment


Feature				StatefulSet 🧠				Deployment ⚡

Pod Identity			Stable						Random

Storage					Persistent					Ephemeral

Scaling					Ordered						Parallel

Use Case				Databases					Web apps


When to Use StatefulSet?

	Use it when your application needs:

	Databases (e.g., MySQL, MongoDB)

	Distributed systems (Kafka, Zookeeper)

	Applications with session/state

	Leader-follower architecture


